text Caching 正式公测 L85: \nL86: 2024年07月01日\nL87: \nL88: ### cite21†Context Caching 如何为 Kimi API 助手节省最高 90% 的调用成本 L89: \nL90: 2024年07月01日\nL91: \nL92: ### cite22†Kimi API 助手的氮气加速装置 —— 以 Golang 为例实践 Context Caching L93: \nL94: 2024年06月26日\nL95: \nL96: ### cite23†用得起的长文本 L97: \nL98: 2024年06月19日\nL99: \nL100: ### cite24†Kimi API 还没用起来？请看这篇无门槛快速入门指南 L101: \nL102: 2024年05月30日\nL103: \nL104: ### cite25†Kimi 大模型 API 更新了，也期待在「亚马逊云科技中国峰会」见到大家 | 开发者速递 L105: \nL106: 2024年05月29日\nL107: \nL108: 2025 © Moonshot AIcite26†用户中心†platform.moonshot.cn cite27†文档†platform.moonshot.cn --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://coefficientgiving.org/wp-content/uploads/Imitation_Learning_Safe_ready.pdf)\nciteturn2559view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2557view0\",\"id\":40}); Total lines: 620\nL0@P0: Imitation Learning is Probably Existentially Safe\nL1@P0: Michael K. Cohen\nL2@P0: University of California, Berkeley\nL3@P0: mkcohen@berkeley.edu\nL4@P0: Marcus Hutter\nL5@P0: Google DeepMind\nL6@P0: www.hutter1.net\nL7@P0: Abstract\nL8@P0: Concerns about extinction risk from AI vary among experts in the field. But AI\nL9@P0: encompasses a very broad category of algorithms. Perhaps some algorithms would\nL10@P0: pose an extinction risk, and others wouldn’t. Such an observation might be of\nL11@P0: great interest to both regulators and innovators. This paper argues that advanced\nL12@P0: imitation learners would likely not cause human extinction. We first present a\nL13@P0: simple argument to that effect, and then we rebut six different arguments that have\nL14@P0: been made to the contrary. A common theme of most of these arguments is a\nL15@P0: story for how a subroutine within an advanced imitation learner could hijack the\nL16@P0: imitation learner’s behavior toward its own ends. But we argue that each argument\nL17@P0: is flawed and each story implausible.\nL18@P0: 1 Introduction\nL19@P0: While many theorists have come to share the view that sufficiently advanced AI systems might pose\nL20@P0: a threat to the continued existence of humanity [Hinton et al., 2023, Cohen et al., 2022, Russell,\nL21@P0: 2019, Bostrom, 2014], it is important, if we are to make progress in thinking about this issue, to be\nL22@P0: clear about which types of AI pose the genuine threats. That way we can focus on where the danger\nL23@P0: actually lies. This paper aims to refute claims that imitation learning algorithms present such a threat.\nL24@P0: While we do think there are types of AI we should be worried about, that does not extend to all types\nL25@P0: of AI. So in what follows, we will examine arguments that have been put forward that imitation\nL26@P0: learners present an extinction risk to humanity, and explain why we think they go wrong.\nL27@P0: First, we’ll offer a simple argument that a sufficiently advanced supervised learning algorithm, trained\nL28@P0: to imitate humans, would very likely not gain total control over humanity (to the point of making\nL29@P0: everyone defenseless) and then cause or allow human extinction from that position.\nL30@P0: No human has ever gained total control over humanity. It would be a very basic mistake to think\nL31@P0: anyone ever has. Moreover, if they did so, very few humans would accept human extinction. An imi\u0002tation learner that successfully gained total control over humanity and then allowed human extinction\nL32@P0: would, on both counts, be an extremely poor imitation of any human, and easily distinguishable from\nL33@P0: one, whereas an advanced imitation learner will likely imitate humans well.\nL34@P0: This basic observation should establish that any conclusion to the contrary should be very surprising,\nL35@P0: and so a high degree of rigor should be expected from arguments to that effect. If a highly advanced\nL36@P0: supervised learning algorithm is directed to the task of imitating a human, then powerful forces of\nL37@P0: optimization are seeking a target that is fundamentally existentially safe: indistinguishability from\nL38@P0: humans. Stories about how such optimization might fail should be extremely careful in establishing\nL39@P0: the plausibility of every step.\nL40@P0: In this paper, we’ll rebut six different arguments we’ve encountered that a sufficiently advanced\nL41@P0: supervised learning algorithm, trained to imitate humans, would likely cause human extinction. These\nL42@P0: arguments originate from Yudkowsky [2008] (the Attention Director Argument), Christiano [2016]\nL43@P0: (the Cartesian Demon Argument), Krueger [2019] (the Simplicity of Optimality Argument), Branwen\nL44@P0: [2022] (the Character Destiny Argument), Yudkowsky [2023] (the Rational Subroutine Argument),\nL45@P0-1: Under review.and Hubinger et al. [2019] (the Deceptive Alignment Argument). Note: Christiano only thinks his\nL46@P1: argument is possibly correct, rather than likely correct, for the advanced AI systems that we will end\nL47@P1: up creating. And Branwen does not think his hypothetical is likely, only plausible enough to discuss.\nL48@P1: But maybe some of the hundreds of upvoters on the community blog LessWrong consider it likely.\nL49@P1: In all cases, we have rewritten the arguments originating from those sources (some of which are\nL50@P1: spread over many pages with gaps that need to be filled in). For Christiano [2016] and Hubinger et al.\nL51@P1: [2019], our rewritten versions of their arguments are shorter, but the longer originals are no stronger\nL52@P1: at the locations that we contest. And for the other four sources, the original text is no thorougher than\nL53@P1: our characterization of their argument. None of the arguments have been peer reviewed, and to our\nL54@P1: knowledge, only Hubinger et al. [2019] was reviewed even informally prior to publication. However,\nL55@P1: we can assure the reader they are taken seriously in many circles.\nL56@P1: 2 Attention Director Argument\nL57@P1: First, we’ll argue that Yudkowsky’s [2008] Attention Director Argument fails. This argument is:\nL58@P1: 1. Learned imitation is the prediction of what actions a human would take.\nL59@P1: 2. Really high-quality prediction sometimes requires making decisions about where to direct\nL60@P1: attention and what thoughts to think.\nL61@P1: 3. These choices must be oriented toward a goal—the goal being to come up with an accurate\nL62@P1: prediction.\nL63@P1: 4. Most goals are best accomplished by first gaining complete control over humanity, if possible,\nL64@P1: and directing all available resources toward the goal.\nL65@P1: 5. The goal-oriented attention-director will recognize this possibility, and do so.\nL66@P1: We contend that the “most” in point 4 is too sloppy, and so it fails to apply to this setting, and point 5\nL67@P1: also fails. First, note that point 4 is less strong than it may appear, because it is most plausible when\nL68@P1: the goal is over the long term, whereas making an accurate prediction is quite a short-term goal. But\nL69@P1: the rest of our counterargument will focus on more definite gaps in the Attention Director Argument.\nL70@P1: Call the goal-oriented attention-director the “subagent”. The structure of our argument is as follows,\nL71@P1: although some points will involve some back and forth:\nL72@P1: A. Goal-directed agents, of which the subagent is one, must have beliefs about the consequences\nL73@P1: of their actions.\nL74@P1: B. If one model of the consequences of a subagent’s actions is much simpler than another, and\nL75@P1: both are viable for understanding the consequences of past behavior, the subagent is unlikely\nL76@P1: to take the much more complex model seriously. (Occam’s razor)\nL77@P1: C. Yudkowsky [2008] implicitly assumes the subagent will have a model (Model M) in which\nL78@P1: its actions control the computation of a computer located on planet Earth, but there is another\nL79@P1: much simpler model (Model N).\nL80@P1: D. Let Model N only model the effects of the actions within a simple computational environ\u0002ment, and let it not model any further effects of actions after the computation following that\nL81@P1: action is complete.\nL82@P1: E. The subagent’s actions will have never before had any visible effect outside the simple\nL83@P1: computational environment, before the computation following that action is complete.\nL84@P1: F. The simpler Model N is viable for understanding the consequences of past behavior.\nL85@P1: G. The subagent will not understand its actions to affect the outside world.\nL86@P1: We will not offer detailed defenses of points A and B, since we believe it is considered common\nL87@P1: knowledge that they are prerequisite for goal attainment. The “beliefs” mentioned in point A need not\nL88@P1: be contained in a dedicated subroutine (as it is for a “model-based” agent). So we begin by detailing\nL89@P1: Model N. Note that the subagent lives in a simple computational environment: the computation that\nL90@P1: the predictor executes to make predictions. So let Model N be a model in which the effects of the\nL91@P1: subagent’s actions are restricted to such a simple computational environment. The subagent is trying\nL92@P1: to promote the predictive accuracy of the predictor, so the subagent’s model of the consequences of\nL93@P1-2: 2its actions must output an estimate of predictive accuracy given the possible actions that it could take;\nL94@P2: then it can use is model to compare actions according to that criterion. Model N is depicted in Figure\nL95@P2: 1 (a); subfigures (b) and (c) are discussed later.\nL96@P2: Figure 1: (a) In the simple computational environment in Model N, “attention choice” is a variable\nL97@P2: whose value can easily be set to equal whatever action the subagent is considering. (b) Planet Earth\nL98@P2: naturally includes the computer running the imitation learner, on which the subagent’s actual actions\nL99@P2: are executed. (c) In Model M, to understand the effect on planet Earth of any and all of the subagent’s\nL100@P2: potential actions, surgery must be done on a model of Earth: the state of a particular computer must\nL101@P2: be modified to match the action under consideration. Note that Model M also includes side-channel\nL102@P2: interactions of the computer with the outside world.\nL103@P2: We now need to defend points E and F and the assertion that Model N is simpler than Model M.\nL104@P2: We start with point E, which asserts: the subagent’s actions will have never before had any visible\nL105@P2: effect outside the simple computational environment before the computation following that action\nL106@P2: is complete. Recall that the subagent’s actions shapes the computation that is executed to predict\nL107@P2: human actions. Computers are designed to specifications: the physical machine’s states must proceed\nL108@P2: according to the encoded computation. If machines were built perfectly, it would follow logically\nL109@P2: that the outside world is disconnected from the subagent’s actions until the computation completes\nL110@P2: and produces an output, as E asserts. In reality, it may be possible for side channels to allow the\nL111@P2: computation to impact the outside world, if certain computations cause the hardware specifications\nL112@P2: to break, but if the subagent does cause this to happen, there has to be a first time, so let’s consider\nL113@P2: whether it is plausible for it to happen the first time. Prior to the first time, side channel effects\nL114@P2: are very unlikely to have appeared by accident, because computers are laboriously constructed so\nL115@P2: that their hardware specifications are robust under normal activity. Therefore, E very likely holds at\nL116@P2: least at that point in time. Assuming the rest of our argument goes through, the subagent will not\nL117@P2-3: 3understand its actions to affect the outside world, so it will have no reason to seek out and exploit\nL118@P3: side channels, and so such side channels are unlikely to be exploited for the first time.\nL119@P3: To defend point F, we look at the properties of Model N defined in point D, and check that they are\nL120@P3: consistent with accurate modelling. Note that the subagent has no need to model any consequences of\nL121@P3: its actions that occur after the computation is complete, because its goal is to cause the computation\nL122@P3: to output an accurate prediction of human behavior. Anything that occurs after this prediction is\nL123@P3: made is irrelevant to its goal. So assuming point E holds, Model N’s restriction of focus to a simple\nL124@P3: computational environment, which persists only until the computation completes, is viable. As a side\nL125@P3: note, a subagent with certain properties can only be expected to exist inasmuch as its existence, with\nL126@P3: those properties, would be useful to the predictor, and modelling the effects of its actions that occur\nL127@P3: after it has done its job for the predictor would be a waste of effort.\nL128@P3: Now we argue that Model N is likely much simpler than Model M. The claims and counterclaims\nL129@P3: (with ′s) are as follows:\nL130@P3: I. Model M must model how the subagent’s actions affect the outside world despite never\nL131@P3: having observed such an interaction, and this extra component makes Model M much more\nL132@P3: complex.\nL133@P3: I\nL134@P3: ′\nL135@P3: . No, the subagent will have a good understanding of planet Earth in order to predict human\nL136@P3: actions, and\nL137@P3: J\nL138@P3: ′\nL139@P3: . Planet Earth includes the subagent and the way its actions affect the outside world, so it\nL140@P3: need not be added as an “extra component” to Model M.\nL141@P3: K. Contra J′\nL142@P3: , models must be able to explain the consequences of counterfactual actions (actions\nL143@P3: that would not actually be taken), and even if planet Earth includes the subagent taking its\nL144@P3: actual actions, it does not include counterfactual actions.\nL145@P3: L. Also contra J′, the input/output behavior of a world-model (including the effects of actions)\nL146@P3: can never be a fully determined logical consequence of the internal dynamics of the world\u0002model.\nL147@P3: Figure 1 (b) depicts a corrected version of point J′, clarifying the distinction between actual actions\nL148@P3: and counterfactual actions, and (c) illustrates Model M, which requires input/output handling, unlike\nL149@P3: planet Earth itself. We’ll first clarify point I, then expand on point K, and then give a background\nL150@P3: discussion of world-models, which is necessary to defend point L.\nL151@P3: To clarify point I, the difference between Model N and Model M is not whether they model (i.e. have\nL152@P3: an understanding of) the outside world; both do, and both must, because the subagent is responsible\nL153@P3: for directing computation in order to successfully imitate humans who are part of the outside world.\nL154@P3: The difference is that only Model M has to model how the subagent’s actions affect the outside world.\nL155@P3: To expand on point K, when an agent uses a model of the consequences of its actions in order to\nL156@P3: select actions, that model must be able to give a verdict about any action under consideration; that is\nL157@P3: the means by which the agent is able to judge which actions are better than which others. Once point\nL158@P3: J\nL159@P3: ′\nL160@P3: is edited for clarity—“Planet Earth includes the subagent and the way its actual actions that are\nL161@P3: actually taken affect the outside world”—point K explains why J′ now fails to contradict point I.\nL162@P3: We now step back to discuss world-models in enough generality to establish point L. The real world\nL163@P3: does not have inputs or outputs. But world-models do have inputs and outputs. When an agent uses\nL164@P3: a world-model to select actions, we are talking about world-models that take the agent’s actions as\nL165@P3: inputs and produce the agent’s observations as outputs. (Alternatively, it can output both actions and\nL166@P3: observations stochastically, and then an agent can understand the consequences of different actions\nL167@P3: by conditioning on different actions being output). This difference between worlds and world-models\nL168@P3: is fundamental, unavoidable, and unproblematic.\nL169@P3: World-models can and must have inputs and outputs. This is not a mistaken, dualistic, philosophical\nL170@P3: hang-up. This is a fundamental entailment of acting at all. If actions are to be evaluated, they must\nL171@P3: be inputs to or outputs of some function. This function is what we call a world-model. It must\nL172@P3: have input/output behavior; that’s what functions do. Again, this is a requirement of acting using\nL173@P3-4: 4a world-model, not an expression of a mistaken philosophy that an enlightened understanding of\nL174@P4: embeddedness could solve.\nL175@P4: The input and output of a world-model are unphysical; that is, nothing in physics (the rules by which\nL176@P4: the world-model’s computation state evolves) fixes the rules by which inputs affect and outputs are\nL177@P4: affected by the computation state of the world-model, and that is unproblematic. Many may feel an\nL178@P4: aesthetic discomfort about the existence of any unphysical computations within a world-model for\nL179@P4: the purpose of governing input- and output-handling, but aesthetic discomfort is a weak argument\nL180@P4: against viability, especially when examples of successful world-models (with inputs and outputs, of\nL181@P4: course) abound.\nL182@P4: Recall that J′claims that the effects on planet Earth of the subagent’s actions are already baked\nL183@P4: into any sufficiently good understanding of planet Earth, not something whose addition is a source\nL184@P4: of complexity. However, as we have established in point L, the physics of a world-model (the\nL185@P4: rules by which computation states evolve) are never sufficient to determine the unphysical rules of\nL186@P4: how the inputs of a world-model affect the world-model’s computation state, or how the outputs\nL187@P4: are determined. The output of a world-model is certainly limited by facts about the physics of a\nL188@P4: world-model, but it is not fully determined by it. Hooking up actions as inputs to or outputs of a\nL189@P4: world-model is always a source of complexity. This point is illustrated with the red arrow in Figure 1\nL190@P4: (c). This completes our argument.\nL191@P4: 3 Cartesian Demon Argument\nL192@P4: Now, we’ll argue that Christiano’s [2016] Cartesian Demon Argument fails. We have named it this\nL193@P4: because of its connection to Descartes’ worry that one explanation for his lifetime of observations is\nL194@P4: that they have been deliberately set up for him by some intelligent demon, who operates outside the\nL195@P4: realm of what he can see. This argument is:\nL196@P4: 1. Learned imitation is the prediction of what actions a human would take.\nL197@P4: 2. Good prediction about a complex world requires considering many complex models of the\nL198@P4: world to be a priori plausible (not necessarily through explicit enumeration).\nL199@P4: 3. Good predictors focus on models that accurately retrodict past observations.\nL200@P4: 4. Some of these accurate models will explain those past observations as follows: they have\nL201@P4: been broadcast by some non-human intelligent beings (like Descartes’ demon) who decided\nL202@P4: to broadcast those observations on the off-chance that the intelligent beings were being\nL203@P4: simulated by a predictor interested in retrodicting those exact observations. (A different\nL204@P4: rationale than Descartes’ demon).\nL205@P4: 5. By randomizing their behavior, the intelligent beings could luck into broadcasting the exact\nL206@P4: observations that the predictor is trying to retrodict.\nL207@P4: 6. Intelligent beings would have an interest in this because:\nL208@P4: (a) Setting up the broadcasts could be cheap.\nL209@P4: (b) Doing so allows their civilization to be deemed plausible by the predictor simulating\nL210@P4: them.\nL211@P4: (c) That allows them to influence the simulator’s world (our world) by broadcasting well\u0002timed “mistakes”—outputs that do not resemble human behavior.\nL212@P4: (d) Whatever their interests, they could try to select these mistakes to cause the construction\nL213@P4: of an AI that takes complete control over humanity and directs all available resources\nL214@P4: to serve the interests of the intelligent beings.\nL215@P4: 7. Call the explanation of data described in points 4-6 the Intelligent Being Hypothesis: “maybe\nL216@P4: this data has been broadcast by intelligent beings, and maybe the data that they (randomly)\nL217@P4: chose to broadcast was certain human behavior on planet Earth”. A predictor should take\n"
